# Is GDP the right measure of how well we live?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What does GDP leave out — housework, leisure, nature, inequality? Should we replace it, or add to it?"
next: {"title":"What do we know so far, and what don't we?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/12-what-do-we-know-so-far-and-what-don-t-we.cast"}

## Income or welfare?
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - United States
      - France
      - Russia
      - Brazil
    values:
      - [100, 67, 37, 17]
      - [100, 92, 21, 11]
    stage: 0
    value_labels: true
    bar_colors: same
    y_label: US = 100
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 110
    box: full
sources:
    - id: jk2016
      title: Beyond GDP? Welfare across Countries and Time
      authors: Charles I. Jones and Peter J. Klenow
      year: 2016
      finding: 'Table 2 (household-survey data; France 2005): GDP per person vs consumption-equivalent welfare, US = 100 — France 67.2 vs 91.8, Russia 37.0 vs 20.7, Brazil 17.2 vs 11.1. France: life expectancy 80.1 vs 77.4, hours worked per person 535 vs 877, lower consumption inequality.'
      url: https://web.stanford.edu/~chadj/rawls.pdf

    text cue_gdp "GDP per person" at.percent [55,92] cites ["jk2016"] hidden true
    text cue_wel "welfare" at.percent [55,92] cites ["jk2016"] hidden true
    scratch parts top-right work ["consumption","+ life","+ leisure","+ equality"] font_size 26 hidden true
    scale guess cites ["jk2016"] y 520 label "France's welfare (US = 100)" min 0 max 100 value 92 ticks 5 hidden true
    scratch blind top-right work ["blind to:","time","health","sharing"] font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Income or welfare?"

Is GDP the right measure of how well we live? Take a test case: France against the United States.
    draw axes cue_gdp bar_1

In 2005, France's GDP  (@draw bar_2@)per person was only about two-thirds of America's: sixty-seven, if America is a hundred.

B: And Russia at thirty-seven, Brazil at seventeen.
    draw bar_3 bar_4 parallel true

B: So the average French person lives a third worse than the average American?
    highlight bar_1 bar_2

    pause 0.3

That's what the bars seem to say. The economists Charles Jones and Peter Klenow asked a sharper question: in which country would you rather live a life, drawn at random?
    highlight bar_2 effect circle

Their measure s (@draw parts_box parts_line_1@)tarts from what people consume, then (@draw parts_line_2@) adds how long they live, how much of (@draw parts_line_3@) their time is their own, and how eq (@draw parts_line_4@)ually it's all shared.

    erase parts cue_gdp

    animate box bottom duration 1.2

    draw guess

    ask "Click your guess on the line." on guess store g default "50" tolerance 0.06 stage own voice b
        intro: So where does France land on that scale?
        right: Close: you said {g}, and it is {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; it is {g.true}.

    erase guess

    animate box full duration 1.2

    draw cue_wel

Here are the bars, measured as welfare. France jumps to ninety-two: nearly level with America.
    animate stage 1 duration 3 ghost true

B: From sixty-seven to ninety-two? What buys twenty-five points?
    highlight bar_2

The French lived almost three years longer, worked far fewer hours, and shared what they consumed more equally. Each of the three lifts France.
    highlight bar_2 effect box

B: And Russia and Brazil go the other way: twenty-one and eleven.
    highlight bar_3 bar_4

Shorter lives in both, and in Brazil deep inequality.
    highlight bar_3 bar_4 effect underline

Pause and slide between GDP and welfare yourself: watch who rises and who falls.
    explore params ["stage"]

So GDP per person is blind to (@draw blind_box blind_line_1@) three things we care about: time,  (@draw blind_line_2@)health, an (@draw blind_line_3@)d how the pi (@draw blind_line_4@)e is shared.

    pause 0.6

## Work it cannot see
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - '1965'
      - '2010'
    series:
      - {name: measured GDP, values: [100, 100]}
      - {name: + housework, values: [[0, 0], [39, 0], [39, 26]]}
    stacked: true
    stage: 0
    value_labels: true
    y_label: US GDP = 100
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 175
    box:
      x: 60
      'y': 110
      w: 880
      h: 540
sources:
    - id: bea2012
      title: Accounting for Household Production in the National Accounts, 1965–2010
      authors: Benjamin Bridgman, Andrew Dugan, Mikhael Lal, Matthew Osborne and Shaunda Villones (BEA, Survey of Current Business)
      year: 2012
      finding: Including nonmarket household production raises nominal US GDP by 39% in 1965 and 26% in 2010; women's home production fell from 40 to 26 hours a week.
      url: https://apps.bea.gov/scb/pdf/2012/05%20May/0512_household.pdf
    - id: pigou1920
      title: The Economics of Welfare
      authors: A. C. Pigou
      year: 1920
      finding: '"Thus, if a man marries his housekeeper or his cook, the national dividend is diminished." (Part I, ch. III)'
      url: https://www.econlib.org/library/NPDBooks/Pigou/pgEW.html?chapter_num=5

    portrait pigou cites ["pigou1920"] x 900 y 590 width 140 of "Arthur Cecil Pigou" hidden true
    scratch hours cites ["bea2012"] work ["women's housework","40 → 26 h a week"] x 800 y 430 font_size 26 hidden true
    scratch fish work ["catch 120 t","regrowth 100 t","GDP counts: 120 t","stock −20 t"] x 800 y 430 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Work it cannot see"

So GDP misses time, health and fairness. But surely it counts all the work we do?
    draw axes bar_1 bar_2

B: Not the work done at home. Cooking, cleaning, looking after children: unpaid, so uncounted.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2

B: Arthur Pigou saw the paradox in 1920: if a man marries his housekeeper, and she keeps doing the same work (@highlight bar_1 bar_2@) unpaid, the national income falls.
    draw pigou

    erase pigou

Same meals, same clean house. What could all that home work be worth? Five percent of GDP?
    point at.ref bar_1 at.anchor top gesture tap

    pause 0.3

    draw legend

B: Far more. American statisticians (@animate stage 1 duration 2.5@) put a value on it: in 1965 it would have added thirty-nine percent to GDP.

B: In 2010, twenty-six percent.
    animate stage 2 duration 2

Why would it shrink?
    animate box.x 70 box.y 110 box.w 560 box.h 530 duration 1.5

B: (@draw hours_box hours_line_1@) Women's housework fell from a (@draw hours_line_2@)bout forty hours a week to twenty-six, as more of them took paid jobs. Some of that work just moved onto the books.

    erase hours

So part of the growth we measured was dinner moving from the kitchen to the restaurant.
    highlight bar_2

    pause 0.3

And nature? Say, with illustrative numbers, Harbourtown' (@draw fish_box fish_line_1@)s fishers land a hundred and twenty tonnes, while the s (@draw fish_line_2@)tock only regrows a hundred.

B: GDP counts all hundred and twenty tonnes as output. Nowhere does it subtract the twenty  (@draw fish_line_4@)tonnes of fish that are gone for good.
    draw fish_line_3

That's the G in GDP: gross. A country can run down its forests and its fish and look richer while it does.
    highlight fish_line_4 effect circle

    pause 0.6

## Replace it, or add to it?
sources:
    - id: jk2016
      title: Beyond GDP? Welfare across Countries and Time
      authors: Charles I. Jones and Peter J. Klenow
      year: 2016
      finding: Across countries, consumption-equivalent welfare and GDP per person have a correlation of 0.98; the standard deviation of log consumption is 0.538 in the US and 0.422 in France, and with lognormal consumption lower inequality adds .5(.538² − .422²) = 5.6% to French welfare. Norway's welfare (81) is far below its income (113) because of its very low consumption share.
      url: https://web.stanford.edu/~chadj/rawls.pdf
    - id: ssf2009
      title: Report by the Commission on the Measurement of Economic Performance and Social Progress
      authors: Joseph E. Stiglitz, Amartya Sen and Jean-Paul Fitoussi
      year: 2009
      finding: Recommends looking at household income and consumption rather than production, giving more prominence to their distribution, broadening income measures to non-market activities, and assessing sustainability with a separate dashboard of indicators.
    - id: kuznets1934
      title: National Income, 1929–1932 (73rd Congress, Senate Document 124)
      authors: Simon Kuznets
      year: 1934
      finding: '"The welfare of a nation can scarcely be inferred from a measurement of national income."'

    scratch rep work ["REPLACE","one number:","welfare index","HDI","weights?"] x 225 y 410 font_size 38 hidden true
    scratch add work ["ADD","GDP + a dashboard:","income & its spread","unpaid work","sustainability"] x 640 y 410 font_size 38 hidden true
    text corr "welfare vs GDP: r = 0.98" cites ["jk2016"] x 500 y 175 font_size 28 hidden true
    portrait kuznets cites ["kuznets1934"] x 915 y 410 width 130 of "Simon Kuznets" hidden true
    text sure1 "GDP ≠ welfare: consensus" x 500 y 165 font_size 28 hidden true
    text sure2 "contested" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 640 font_size 30 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch math cites ["jk2016"] work [{"tex":"\\text{welfare} \\approx \\log \\bar{c} - \\tfrac{1}{2}\\sigma^2"},"spread σ:  US 0.538,  France 0.422",{"tex":"\\tfrac{1}{2}(0.538^2 - 0.422^2) \\approx 0.056"},"≈ +5.6 % for France"] x 500 y 420 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Replace it, or add to it?"

So GDP misses time, health, fairness, housework and nature. Should we throw it out?
    draw rep_box rep_line_1 add_box add_line_1 parallel true

B: I'm tempted. Politicians chase the head (@draw rep_line_2 rep_line_3@)line number, so give them a better one.

There are candidates: the welfare measure we just saw, or the UN's  (@draw rep_line_4@)Human Development Index, which mixes income, health and schooling.

B: But any single index has to weigh health against income ag (@draw rep_line_5@)ainst schooling. Who picks the weights?

Which is the case for adding instead. In 2009 a commission led by Joseph Stiglitz and Amartya Sen urged looking beyo (@draw add_line_2 add_line_3@)nd GDP: at household income, its distribut (@draw add_line_4@)ion, unpaid work, and a dashboar (@draw add_line_5@)d for sustainability.

B: And GDP still does its own job well: it tracks production, jobs and tax revenue through booms and busts.
    highlight add_line_2

    pause 0.3

B: Here's the twist, though. Across countries, Jones and Klenow's welfare and GDP per person go to (@draw corr@)gether almost perfectly: a correlation of zero point nine eight.

So between rich and poor, GDP gets the order right. It misleads in the finer comparisons, like France against America.
    highlight corr effect underline

    erase corr

How sure are we? T (@draw sure1@)hat GDP is not welfare: consensus. Simon Kuzne (@draw kuznets@)ts, who built the first American national accounts, warned of it in 1934. Whether to replace (@draw sure2@) it or add to it: contested.

    erase kuznets

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want to see how inequality enters the welfare measure?

@math

    erase mathq

    fade rep add sure1 sure2 to 0.12

B: If consumption is spread in the usual lopsided way, average well-being is the log of average consumption, minus half the variance of its log.
    draw math_box math_line_1

B: That spread is about zero point five four in America and zero point four two in France. With the exact figures, half the difference of t (@draw math_line_3@)heir squares is about five and a half percent more welfar (@draw math_line_4@)e for France, from equality alone.
    draw math_line_2

    erase math

@test

    erase mathq

    fade rep add sure1 sure2 to 1

    hide rep add sure1 sure2

    cards gdpsort
        Counted in GDP: Restaurant meal
        Left out: Cooking at home
        Counted in GDP: Rebuilding after a storm
        Left out: A day off
        Counted in GDP: Fish the stock can't replace
        Left out: Living longer

    ask "Which of these does GDP count? Sort the cards." intro "Now some new cases." on gdpsort store srt stage own voice b
        right: All {srt.total} right: GDP counts what is paid for.
        wrong: You got {srt} of {srt.total}: GDP counts what is sold.

    erase gdpsort

    show rep add sure1 sure2

    quiz "Norway puts most of its oil income into a fund instead of spending it. On Jones and Klenow's welfare measure, Norway looks…"
        right: Poorer: its people consume a small share of what the country earns, so its welfare is about 81 against an income of 113 (US = 100).
        wrong: The measure counts what people consume, not what the country earns.
        * richer than its GDP
        * about as rich as its GDP
        + poorer than its GDP

    quiz "After a hurricane, a town spends a year rebuilding the houses it lost. What happens to its GDP that year?" voice b
        right: It rises: rebuilding is paid work, though the town is only getting back what it had.
        wrong: Rebuilding is paid work, whether or not anyone is better off than before the storm.
        + It rises
        * It falls
        * It can't be told

So GDP measures what an economy produces, and roughly how well its people live. For the rest, one number is not enough.
    highlight rep_line_1 add_line_1

    pause 0.8

